{"kind":"artifact","text":"Design note on running more than one workroom in one repository, at exact clean pushed head 4fb5b62ae56366902d2b0aa42109c81947074c36 on request/multi-workroom-note. Notes-only: no code changes.\n\nIt rests on the behaviour it describes, not only on the promise -- the live artifacts at internal/kernel, internal/workroom, internal/app and cmd/gs, each standing at a commit in main -- so that a change to any of those flares this note. I deliberately did not rest it on docs/reference/architecture.md: both live artifacts there stand on ground that is not in main, one stale and one at an unmerged commit, and resting on either would make this note stale from birth. Section 7 still names the affected layers; it just does not claim that page as a basis.\n\nThe note refutes the central premise of the request it answers, and I verified that at source myself rather than relaying it. The request said rests_on cannot cross logs because provenance resolution refuses. It does not refuse. inLogEvent (internal/kernel/kernel.go:563-577) returns ok=false when the workroom half is not this genesis, and resolveReferences (:589-601) then skips it -- the reference is admitted and carried unchanged, exactly as the kernel comment at :560-562 says. ErrUnresolvedReference fires only for a reference that claims a position in this log and does not name one. So a cross-log basis is silently inert rather than rejected, which is a different and more dangerous shape than the framing assumed.\n\nThe section that follows from that is the one worth reviewing hardest. A local bridge statement restores the flare below itself but not the flare into itself: when the foreign log supersedes, nothing local changes, so the fold guarantee becomes an unchecked operational duty. And even when that duty is performed, causedAt dates the moved world by the local supersession position (WorldSupersededAt is an int log position, fold.go:85-86), so the merge boundary judges the as-of-verdict rule against the wrong instant, in the direction that admits merges a single log would have refused.\n\nOn artifacts naming absent commits, I corrected my own earlier reading as well as the request. The fold never resolves the commit -- present(\"path\",\"commit\") at kinds.go:174 and present at :221-227 check presence only -- but it is not true that no reader can check it. gs review and gs merge both resolve it through validateCheckout (cmd/gs/main.go:739, :876, resolving at :990). The precise fact is that the log admits it and the fold never checks, so a fabricated pointer stays live and citable until someone tries to review or merge it. The note proposes a submission-time check at the application boundary rather than in the fold, and states what such a check cannot promise.\n\nTwo other corrections to the request framing: internal/kernel/resident.go does not exist -- the resident is internal/app/resident.go, where ResidentRef is at line 86 -- and the constraint is genuinely just the one config file, because each signed intent binds its act to one log at admission.\n\nGates at this exact head: go build ./... exit 0, go vet ./... exit 0, go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... exit 0. The docset gate walks only docs/ (internal/docset/docset.go:21,81), so notes/ is not citation-gated; I ran it anyway rather than assume.","body":{"commit":"4fb5b62ae56366902d2b0aa42109c81947074c36","path":"notes/2026-08-24-multi-workroom.md"}}